EVEN RSA CAN BE BROKEN???
APPROACH
At first I was honestly not sure how to solve this one because it only gave n and e with an encrypted flag.
I knew it was RSA but I did not know what could be done with just n and e. So I first looked up how RSA works and how it can be broken if only n and e are given.
I found that n is made by multiplying two prime numbers. If we can find those prime numbers then we can calculate the private key and decrypt the message.
My first thought was to factor n normally. But n was very large so I did not think I could do that easily.
Then I went back to the hints. One hint was about randomness and another one said to notice something about n. I also tried comparing n from different requests because of the last hint. At first I thought maybe some prime number was being reused.
After checking n again I noticed that n was an even number.
That was the main clue. Since 2 is the only even prime number one of the prime numbers had to be 2. So the other prime number could simply be found by doing n / 2.

SOLUTION
I launched the instance and got:
$ nc xebec.cylabacademy.net 45591
I also opened the source code given with the challenge to understand how the RSA key was being made.
The important part was:

e = 65537

def gen_key(k):
    p,q = get_primes(k//2)
    N = p*q
    d = inverse(e, (p-1)*(q-1))

This showed that n is made by multiplying two prime numbers and d is calculated using phi.
Then I tried to connect to the instance in PowerShell.
At first I copied the $ from the command too and got an error:
$ : The term '$' is not recognized as the name of a cmdlet
I did not know what this meant at first. I then realised that the $ was only part of the example terminal prompt.
So I tried: nc xebec.cylabacademy.net 45591
But then PowerShell gave me another error saying:nc : The term 'nc' is not recognized
So I understood that netcat was not installed on my computer.
I then used PowerShell's built in networking instead:
$client = New-Object System.Net.Sockets.TcpClient("xebec.cylabacademy.net",45591)
$stream = $client.GetStream()
$buffer = New-Object byte[] 4096
$n = $stream.Read($buffer,0,$buffer.Length)
[Text.Encoding]::ASCII.GetString($buffer,0,$n)
This let me connect to the service and get n e and the ciphertext.
Then I checked n again and saw that it was even.
So:
n = prime1 × prime2

prime1 = 2
prime2 = n / 2
After finding the two prime numbers I calculated:
phi = (prime1 - 1) × (prime2 - 1)
Since one of them is 2:
phi = prime2 - 1
Then I calculated d:
d = e^(-1) mod phi
After that I decrypted the ciphertext using:
m = c^d mod n
I used PowerShell for the calculations.
$q = $n / 2
$phi = $q - 1
$d = Inv $e $phi
$m = PowMod $c $d $n
After this I got the decrypted value but it was not directly readable text.
I tried converting it using:[Convert]::FromHexString($hex)
but this gave me another error because my PowerShell version did not have that method.
I then changed the conversion and converted the hex two characters at a time:
$bytes = for($i=0;$i -lt $hex.Length;$i+=2){
    [Convert]::ToByte($hex.Substring($i,2),16)
}
[Text.Encoding]::ASCII.GetString($bytes)
This finally gave me the flag.

